  <h3 class="accordion__header is-closed">
<button type="button"
          class="accordion__title is-collapsed aalto-anchor-link-heading aalto-anchor-link-skip-toc"
          aria-expanded="false"
          aria-controls="accordion-content--11"
          id="accordion-title--11"><span class="accordion__title-text">

                    Thesis
            </span></button></h3>
<div class="accordion__content is-collapsed" aria-labelledby="accordion-title--11" id="accordion-content--11">
  
                    <div class="aalto-user-generated-content"><p>JOIN.bsc Bachelor's Thesis and Seminar is a 10 ECTS study module that is completed during one study term in third year of studies. The bachelor’s thesis is written while attending the seminar.</p>
<p>After completing the Bachelor’s Thesis and Seminar you will be able to understand scientific processes and search for relevant background information, both applying and analysing the data collected. In addition, you will be able to produce scientific articles in precise and correct language, present your findings and both receive and give feedback.</p>
<p>The language of instruction of the seminar is English and the thesis is written in English. Students who have been educated in Finnish or Swedish are required to write their maturity essay in the language of educaton (i.e. Finnish or Swedish).</p>
<p>More information on the Bachelor's Thesis and Seminar will be provided to students during their studies in the programme.</p></div>
            
  </div>

  <h3 class="accordion__header is-closed">
<button type="button"
          class="accordion__title is-collapsed aalto-anchor-link-heading aalto-anchor-link-skip-toc"
          aria-expanded="false"
          aria-controls="accordion-content--12"
          id="accordion-title--12"><span class="accordion__title-text">

                    Minor 20–25 ECTS
            </span></button></h3>
<div class="accordion__content is-collapsed" aria-labelledby="accordion-title--12" id="accordion-content--12">
  
                    <div class="aalto-user-generated-content"><p>A minor subject of 20–25 credits is a compulsory part of a bachelor's degree. .The combined extent of the minor (20–25 cr) and the elective studies in Computational Engineering (23-28 ECTS) is 48 ECTS. The minor is confirmed in the student’s personal study plan (HOPS). It is possible to <a href="/en/minors#/">choose a minor within Aalto University</a>, from another Finnish university through <a href="/en/node/799451">the Flexible Study Right (JOO) agreement</a> or from university abroad during a student exchange.</p>
<p>Minor studies must be university level subject studies and they must form a module. It is not possible to take individual courses or language courses as a minor. Also studies in the field of student's major and courses that are overlapping with other studies are excluded from the minor selection.</p>
<h3>Minor from Aalto University</h3>
<p>Minor subjects offered by the different disciplines at Aalto University are listed on <a href="/en/minors#/">Minors.</a> The minor modules of Aalto University Language Centre are not suitable as actual minors.</p>
<p>Currently, minors that are offered in English and are suitable for bachelor's level students include at least the following:</p>
<ul>
<li>Bioinformation Technology (School of ELEC)</li>
<li>Chemical Engineering&nbsp;(School of CHEM)</li>
<li>Collaborative Product Development (School of ENG)</li>
<li>Computer Engineering (School of ELEC)</li>
<li>Computer Science&nbsp;Y (School of SCI)</li>
<li>Computer Science&nbsp;L (School of SCI)</li>
<li>Data Science&nbsp;(School of SCI)</li>
<li>Digital Systems and Design&nbsp;(School of ELEC)</li>
<li>Economics&nbsp;(School of BIZ)</li>
<li>Finance (School of BIZ)</li>
<li>Information and Service Management&nbsp;(School of BIZ)</li>
<li>Information Technology Programme&nbsp;(School of BIZ)</li>
<li>International Business&nbsp;(School of BIZ)</li>
<li>Mathematics&nbsp;(School of SCI)</li>
<li>Quantitative Methods&nbsp;(School of BIZ)</li>
<li>Quantum Technology&nbsp;(Schools of ELEC and SCI)</li>
<li>Statistics&nbsp;(School of SCI)</li>
</ul></div>
            
  </div>

  <h3 class="accordion__header is-closed">
<button type="button"
          class="accordion__title is-collapsed aalto-anchor-link-heading aalto-anchor-link-skip-toc"
          aria-expanded="false"
          aria-controls="accordion-content--13"
          id="accordion-title--13"><span class="accordion__title-text">

                    Elective Studies 23–28 ECTS
            </span></button></h3>
<div class="accordion__content is-collapsed" aria-labelledby="accordion-title--13" id="accordion-content--13">
  
                    <div class="aalto-user-generated-content"><p>Computational Engineering major students choose 23-28 credits of elective studies. The combined extent of the minor and the elective studies is 48 ECTS. Individual elective courses can be taken from other programmes at Aalto University or other Finnish universities through possible RIPA agreements. Studies completed abroad during a student exchange can also be included in the elective studies category (exchange studies can also form an international minor or be included in the major). You can also select courses in the&nbsp;<a href="https://www.unite-university.eu/learn/student-catalogue?sfr">European Unite! universities</a>.</p>
<p>You can also choose an elective minor. The Language Centre's minor subjects are always placed in elective studies.</p>
<p>Elective studies must be university level studies that fulfill the degree requirements and, in general, studies that are offered as degree studies at the university in question.</p>
<p>The elective studies may include a work placement furthering professional development. It is possible to include 1–10 ECTS of work experience in a bachelor’s degree.</p>
<p>Entrepreneurial, multidisciplinary Aalto studies and language and communication courses offered by Aalto University Language Centre are recommended as elective studies.&nbsp;Foreign students are encouraged to take Finnish courses.</p></div>
            
  </div>

</div>
      </div>
  </div>

      </div>

      
  <div class="aalto-component-wrapper aalto-component  aalto-component-wrapper--text-accordion"
        lang="en"       style="">
    
  <div class="paragraph--text-accordion component-container">
      <div class="accordion__container">
                  <h2>  Computer Engineering
</h2>
                        <div class="listing-accordion__container">  <h3 class="accordion__header is-closed">
<button type="button"
          class="accordion__title is-collapsed aalto-anchor-link-heading aalto-anchor-link-skip-toc"
          aria-expanded="false"
          aria-controls="accordion-content--14"
          id="accordion-title--14"><span class="accordion__title-text">

                    Learning outcomes of the major
            </span></button></h3>
<div class="accordion__content is-collapsed" aria-labelledby="accordion-title--14" id="accordion-content--14">
  
                    <div class="aalto-user-generated-content"><p>Computer engineering is a discipline that embodies the science and technology of design, construction, implementation, and maintenance of software and hardware components of modern computing systems, computer-controlled equipment, and networks of intelligent devices. It is an interdisciplinary subject combining electrical engineering and electronics with computer science. Computer engineering harnesses the foundational concepts of computing, mathematics, science, and engineering, channeling them to address technical challenges by designing advanced computing hardware, software, networks, and processes.</p>
<p>Computer engineers increasingly design specialized computer-based systems across various industries—from automotive and aerospace to telecommunications and defense. They create everything from microelectronic chips to powerful interconnected systems and play pivotal roles in distributed computing environments and embedded systems found in vehicles, aircraft, and spacecraft. Many advanced technological infrastructures, including power distribution and modern manufacturing plants, depend on systems crafted by these engineers.</p>
<p>As a student of this major, you will</p>
<ul>
<li>Gain comprehensive understanding on how computer hardware works</li>
<li>Use principles of mathematics, science, and engineering to articulate potential solutions for computer engineering challenges</li>
<li>Develop the requisite mathematical proficiency and mastery of physical theories and engineering methods to master computer engineering from a technological standpoint</li>
<li>Learn to analyse the interdependencies between hardware and software components in a computer system to understand the potential bottlenecks and optimization opportunities</li>
<li>Acquire to the skills to design cutting-edge computers, computer-based systems, and networks.</li>
</ul>
<p>Upon completion of your degree, you will have tools to tackle industrial challenges involving computer-based systems and computing hardware and related software for the benefit of society. &nbsp;Computer Engineering is one of the majors in the Aalto University Bachelor's Programme in Science and Technology and is taught entirely in English.&nbsp;</p></div>
            
  </div>

